The Internet Reference Models and Internet Protocols activity helped me understand how different devices communicate with each other over the Internet. I learned that the Internet uses different protocols to make sure information can be sent and received correctly. I also learned more about TCP/IP and how different layers have different responsibilities when data is being transferred. Before this activity, I knew that my computer connected to the Internet, but I did not really understand what was happening in the background. I can apply this knowledge in my own environment by having a better understanding of how my computer, phone, and other devices communicate when I use websites, apps, or other online services.
The Ping and Traceroute activity helped me understand how to test an Internet connection and see how data travels across a network. I learned that ping can be used to determine whether a device or Internet host is reachable. Traceroute goes a step further by showing the different network hops that data passes through before reaching its destination. This helped me understand that when I visit a website, my information does not travel directly from my computer to the website. It passes through multiple devices and networks along the way. I can apply this knowledge if I experience slow or unreliable Internet because these tools can help identify where a connection problem may be occurring.
The IP Address Settings and Connectivity activity helped me learn more about how devices are identified on a network. I learned that an IP address identifies a device, while the default gateway allows the device to communicate outside of the local network. I also learned how DHCP can automatically provide devices with their network settings. Using network commands such as ifconfig helped me see information about my own network connection. This is something I can use in the future if I have problems connecting to Wi-Fi or the Internet. Instead of just assuming that the Internet is not working, I can check my network settings to help figure out where the problem is.
The Domain Name System and nslookup activity helped me understand how domain names work. I learned that DNS translates easy-to-remember domain names, such as en.wikiversity.org, into IP addresses that computers can use to locate the correct server. The nslookup command can be used to check this process and find information about a domain. This is useful because people do not have to remember long IP addresses every time they want to visit a website. I can apply this concept in the future when troubleshooting a website or network problem. For example, if I can connect to an IP address but cannot reach a website using its domain name, DNS could be part of the problem.
